    "method_card": {
      "title": "Averages from Frequency Tables and Grouped Data",
      "content": "<p>When data are in a frequency table you cannot simply add all values &mdash; you must use the frequency-weighted approach.</p><p>For an <strong>ungrouped frequency table</strong>, the mean is \\(\\bar{x} = \\frac{\\sum fx}{\\sum f}\\). The mode is the value with highest frequency. For the median, count through cumulative frequencies to find the \\(\\frac{\\sum f + 1}{2}\\)th value.</p><p>For a <strong>grouped frequency table</strong>, individual values are hidden inside class intervals, so you use the <strong>midpoint</strong> of each class: \\(\\bar{x} \\approx \\frac{\\sum fm}{\\sum f}\\). This is always an <em>estimate</em> because using midpoints assumes values are evenly spread within each class.</p><p>The <strong>modal class</strong> has the highest frequency. The <strong>median class</strong> contains the \\(\\frac{\\sum f}{2}\\)th value when counting through cumulative frequencies.</p>",
      "steps": [
        "Find midpoints: \\(m = \\frac{\\text{lower} + \\text{upper}}{2}\\) for each class",
        "Multiply each midpoint by its frequency: \\(fm\\)",
        "Sum all \\(fm\\) values to get \\(\\sum fm\\) and all frequencies to get \\(\\sum f\\)",
        "Calculate estimated mean: \\(\\bar{x} \\approx \\frac{\\sum fm}{\\sum f}\\)",
        "Identify the modal class (highest frequency) and median class (contains the \\(\\frac{\\sum f}{2}\\)th value)",
        "State that the mean is an estimate because midpoints are used"
      ]
    },
    "exam_context": {
      "paper": "Paper 1 and Paper 2",
      "marks": "3&ndash;5 marks for a full grouped mean; 1&ndash;2 marks for modal class or median class identification",
      "frequency": "One of the highest-frequency calculation topics; appears on nearly every paper"
